    "question": {
      ":question/id": "6a976437-aa27-582b-8cff-a16f47ffc336",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 9 & -6 \\\\ -3 & 3 & -3 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-6 & 8 & 5 \\\\ -9 & 5 & 6 \\\\ -6 & 7 & 6 \\\\ 7 & 6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$F=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & -4 & -9 \\\\ -2 & -7 & -9 & 5 \\\\ 3 & 5 & -6 & -1 \\\\ -5 & 6 & 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $D,E,$ and $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $D$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $E$ is a $4\\times 3$ matrix, and $F$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $D$ and $4$ rows in $F.$ Since the number of columns in $D$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $F,$ the matrices $D$ and $F$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{D}_{3\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{F}_{4\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $E$ and $4$ rows in $F.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $F,$ the matrices $E$ and $F$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{4\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{F}_{4\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $F$ and $4$ rows in $E.$ Since the number of columns in $F$ equals the number of rows in $E,$ the matrices $F$ and $E$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{F}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{E}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nThe product $FE$ is well defined. The products $DF$ and $EF$ are undefined.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "41c69a8a-c073-5f69-91c8-203d9e5df67f",
          ":answer-field/key": "The product $DF$ is well defined.",
