    "question": {
      ":question/id": "e631422e-6c65-5566-949e-4aca203742fc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -6 & -8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 5 & -9 \\\\ -4 & 2 & -9 & -3 \\\\ -2 & 3 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -7 \\\\ 2 & -8 \\\\ -4 & -7 \\\\ -7 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$F=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & -5 & 5 & 6 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 2 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $D,E,$ and $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $D$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $E$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, and $F$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $D$ and $4$ rows in $E.$ Since the number of columns in $D$ equals the number of rows in $E,$ the matrices $D$ and $E$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{D}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{E}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $E$ and $4$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $E$ and $D$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{4\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{D}_{4\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $D$ and $2$ rows in $F.$ Since the number of columns in $D$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $F,$ the matrices $D$ and $F$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{D}_{4\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{F}_{2\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nThe product $DE$ is well defined. The products $ED$ and $DF$ are undefined.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
