  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100869,
      "id": "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140",
      "title": "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "5d204e2f-37ed-5f45-a3be-3f376aa21109",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$J=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 8 & 4 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -4 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 4 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -3 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 7 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $J,K,$ and $L?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $J$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, $K$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, and $L$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $J$ and $3$ rows in $K.$ Since the number of columns in $J$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $K,$ the matrices $J$ and $K$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{J}_{2\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{K}_{3\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $L$ and $3$ rows in $K.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $K$, the matrices $L$ and $K$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{3\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{K}_{3\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $K$ and $3$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $K$ equals the number of rows in $L$, the matrices $K$ and $L$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{K}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{L}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nThe product $KL$ is well defined. The products $JK$ and $LK$ are undefined.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "21e3269b-87e5-57a7-9ec9-00a36432f676",
          ":answer-field/key": "The product $JK$ is well defined.",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/checkbox",
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "e2ffcd06-999a-5708-a3fd-1e47bca3e88e",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/text",
              ":answer/value": "false"
            },
            {
              ":answer/id": "70ec39cf-eeb2-5f0f-934d-de98383a8ee0",
