    "question": {
      ":question/id": "9083df5e-6a30-54d3-959a-0edc48ed15eb",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -9\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -2 & -3 & 9 \\\\ -9 & -5 & -6 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 6 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 5 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $A$ is a $3\\times 1$ column vector, $B$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $C$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $C$ and $3$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $C$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{C}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 1}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $4$ columns in $B$ and $3$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $B$ and $C$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{2\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{C}_{3\\times 3}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $C$ and $2$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $C$ and $B$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{C}_{3\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{B}_{2\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nThe product $CA$ is well defined. The products $BC$ and $CB$ are undefined.",
