  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100869,
      "id": "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140",
      "title": "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "13013588-9caf-54bb-b80f-9ae0e0cfabfc",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$J=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 6 & 3 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 2 & 4 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 9 \\\\ 7 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -2 & 6 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 9 & -7 \\\\ 9 & -6 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3 & -7\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $J,K,$ and $L?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $J$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $K$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, and $L$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $L$ and $4$ rows in $J.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $J,$ the matrices $L$ and $J$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{3\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{J}_{4\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $3$ columns in $K$ and $3$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $K$ equals the number of rows in $L$, the matrices $K$ and $L$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{K}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{L}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $K$ and $4$ rows in $J.$ Since the number of columns in $K$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $J$, the matrices $K$ and $J$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{K}_{3\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{J}_{4\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nThe product $KL$ is well defined. The products $LJ$ and $KJ$ are undefined.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "8c58f72f-d379-5d23-be7c-f76be27c2737",
          ":answer-field/key": "The product $LJ$ is well defined.",
