      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$J=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 4 & 5 \\\\ 6 & 3 & -4 \\\\ -5 & -7 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 7 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ 7 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -3\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 9 & 0 \\\\ 5 & -1 & 8\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $J,K,$ and $L?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $J$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, $K$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, and $L$ is a $2\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $L$ and $4$ rows in $K.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $K,$ the matrices $L$ and $K$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{2\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{K}_{4\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $K$ and $2$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $K$ equals the number of rows in $L$, the matrices $K$ and $L$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{K}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{L}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $L$ and $3$ rows in $J.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ equals the number of rows in $J$, the matrices $L$ and $J$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{J}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 3}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nThe products $KL$ and $LJ$ are well defined. The product $LK$ is undefined.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
