      ":question/id": "b0a7b089-1142-5fb4-bed8-14bd21c813c2",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$J=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 & 3 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 7 & -1 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -4 & 1 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$K=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -7 & 9 & 2 \\\\ 5 & 3 & -9 & 5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$L=\\begin{bmatrix}5 & -6 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 8 & -4 & 5 & -6 \\\\ -2 & 0 & -6 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $J,K,$ and $L?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $J$ is a $4\\times 4$ matrix, $K$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix, and $L$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nThere are $4$ columns in $J$ and $3$ rows in $L.$ Since the number of columns in $J$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $L,$ the matrices $J$ and $L$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{J}_{4\\times 4}{\\times }_{\\ne }{L}_{3\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $4$ columns in $L$ and $4$ rows in $J.$ Since the number of columns in $L$ equals the number of rows in $J$, the matrices $L$ and $J$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{L}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{J}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $4$ columns in $K$ and $4$ rows in $J.$ Since the number of columns in $K$ equals the number of rows in $J$, the matrices $K$ and $J$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{K}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{J}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nThe products $LJ$ and $KJ$ are well defined. The product $JL$ is undefined.",
