      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$D=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -4 & 7 & 0 \\\\ -9 & -8 & -9 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 1 & -1 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$E=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 6 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$F=\\begin{bmatrix}-8 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 4 & -2 & 4 \\\\ -8 & -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $D,E,$ and $F?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $D$ is a $3\\times 4$ matrix, $E$ is a $2\\times 2$ matrix, and $F$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\nThere are $3$ columns in $F$ and $3$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $F$ equals the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $F$ and $D$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{F}_{3\\times \\boxed{\\text{3}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{D}_{\\boxed{\\text{3}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $2$ columns in $E$ and $3$ rows in $D.$ Since the number of columns in $E$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $D,$ the matrices $E$ and $D$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{E}_{2\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{D}_{3\\times 4}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $3$ columns in $F$ and $2$ rows in $E.$ Since the number of columns in $F$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $E,$ the matrices $F$ and $E$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{F}_{3\\times 3}{\\times }_{\\ne }{E}_{2\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nThe product $FD$ is well defined. The products $ED$ and $FE$ are undefined.",
