  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186100869,
      "id": "b494140f-206a-5317-8079-ff719a253140",
      "title": "Determining Whether Two Matrices Are Conformable for Multiplication"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1e3fc957-87d7-5aa6-82d0-89f5bed6e883",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "<div class=\"matrix-operands\">\n<div>$A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 7 \\\\ -1 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -3 \\\\ 9 & -5\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$B=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -4 \\\\ -8 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n<div>$C=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 9 & -7 & -2 \\\\ -8 & -6 & -6 & -6\\end{bmatrix}$</div>\n</div>\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the matrices $A,B,$ and $C?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that $A$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, $B$ is a $4\\times 2$ matrix, and $C$ is a $2\\times 4$ matrix.\n\nThere are $2$ columns in $A$ and $4$ rows in $B.$ Since the number of columns in $A$ does **not** equal the number of rows in $B,$ the matrices $A$ and $B$ are **not** conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{A}_{4\\times 2}{\\times }_{\\ne }{B}_{4\\times 2}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, there are $4$ columns in $C$ and $4$ rows in $A.$ Since the number of columns in $C$ equals the number of rows in $A,$ the matrices $C$ and $A$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{C}_{2\\times \\boxed{\\text{4}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{A}_{\\boxed{\\text{4}}\\times 2}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nFinally, there are $2$ columns in $B$ and $2$ rows in $C.$ Since the number of columns in $B$ equals the number of rows in $C,$ the matrices $B$ and $C$ are conformable for multiplication:\n\n$$\n{B}_{4\\times \\boxed{\\text{2}}}{\\times }_{\u2713}{C}_{\\boxed{\\text{2}}\\times 4}=\\text{well defined}\n$$\n\nThe products $CA$ and $BC$ are well defined. The product $AB$ is undefined.",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "cdb8d499-394c-53b3-beca-68cf1d6795dd",
